{
  "module": "Position: where tokens are in the sequence",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "rope",
    "title": "Rotary position embedding (RoPE)",
    "summary": "How RoPE builds position into attention itself: query and key vectors are rotated by an angle proportional to position, so the score depends only on how far apart two tokens are.",
    "covers": ["rope"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "position-need",
        "reason": "RoPE is one of the fixes for the problem it describes."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "scores-weights",
        "reason": "It changes the query and key vectors whose dot product gives the scores."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "relative",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "For many purposes what matters is not where a token is in absolute terms, but "
            },
            {
              "text": "how far apart two tokens are",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": the previous word, or three words back."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Rotary position embedding",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", RoPE, is built so that the attention score between two tokens depends on exactly that: their "
            },
            {
              "text": "offset",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 48)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "rotate",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "RoPE takes each query and key vector and treats its entries as consecutive "
            },
            {
              "text": "pairs",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". It "
            },
            {
              "text": "rotates",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " each pair by an angle proportional to the token's position: pos × θ, where the frequencies θᵢ are spaced geometrically across the pairs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Rotating a pair (x, y) by an angle a gives:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "(x, y) \\mapsto (x\\cos a - y\\sin a,\\; x\\sin a + y\\cos a)",
          "spoken": "the pair x, y is mapped to x cosine a minus y sine a, and x sine a plus y cosine a"
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 48)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "offset",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Now compare a query at position m with a key at position n. Rotating the query by m·θ and the key by n·θ makes their dot product depend only on the "
            },
            {
              "text": "offset m − n",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", not on m and n separately."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy check with one pair, θ = 0.5, and both vectors (1, 0) before rotating:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "positions 3 and 1 (offset 2): score 0.540"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "positions 7 and 5 (offset 2): score 0.540"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "positions 3 and 2 (offset 1): score 0.878"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The first two are equal, because the offset is the same. In this case the score is cos(offset × θ): cos(2 × 0.5) = 0.540."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 48)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "where",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "RoPE is applied to the "
            },
            {
              "text": "queries and keys",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", inside "
            },
            {
              "text": "every",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " attention layer. It is "
            },
            {
              "text": "not",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " applied to the values, and it is "
            },
            {
              "text": "not",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " added to the token embeddings."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Only the score q · k sees the rotation. So the attention pattern depends on relative offset, while the value vectors carry content unchanged."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 48, 49)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "no-vector-added",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Unlike adding a position vector to the input, RoPE means "
            },
            {
              "text": "no position vector is added anywhere",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". Instead, each layer rotates Q and K, so position affects only the attention scores, and does so in every layer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "RoPE is used in the Llama family of models, among others."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 48, 50)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
